WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.568
-Our first guest tonight
is an Oscar-, Emmy-,

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:03.770
and Tony-nominated actor

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.938
you know from films
such as "Marriage Story,"

00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:07.841
"House of Gucci,"
and the "Star Wars" franchise.

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:10.844
He stars in "65," which is in
theaters Friday, March 10th.

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:11.877
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:13.379
our friend Adam Driver,
everybody.

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:16.382
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:34.734 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.336
Welcome back!

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:37.804
-Thank you, thank you,
thank you.

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:39.005
-Very, very, very happy
to have you here.

00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:41.541
-Thanks for having me.
-This is one of those

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:44.177
really fun for all ages
blockbusters.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:46.479
I would recommend people see it
in the theater.

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:50.216
Do you remember the first time
you saw one of these movies

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:51.951
with your family
where you got to be a kid

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.620
and go out and see one of these

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.154
really fun things
on a big screen?

00:00:55.154 --> 00:00:58.691
-Yeah, mine was -- I think
"Star Trek" was the first movie.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:01.694
The one they go to a planet
and they think it's a god.

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:03.663
-Oh, I don't remember --
I don't remember --

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:05.331
I'm glad you kept going,
'cause I'm like,

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:06.933
"I think they go to a planet
in all of them."

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.268
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:10.570
I think William Shatner
directed it.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:11.704
-Okay.
-I think it was like

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.273
Leonard Nimoy did the one
with the whales.

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:15.374
-Okay, you're right.
Nimoy was the whales.

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:16.876
-Then after that, I think he
directed the one

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:19.011
where you go
and they think it's god

00:01:19.011 --> 00:01:20.413
and it turns out
to be a false prophet.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:21.981
-Okay, gotcha.

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:24.417
This seems, like, very
complicated for your first one.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:25.885
-That was my first one.

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:28.054
I think I'm getting the dates
right, but I was --

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:30.289
that I remember being the
first one I saw in the theater.

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:31.424
-And do you remember
enjoying it?

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.058
-I mean, yeah,
I had a great time.

00:01:33.058 --> 00:01:35.894
I watched the show, "The Next
Generation," with my dad.

00:01:35.894 --> 00:01:39.331
-Oh, that's great.
-So, I was expecting it to be --

00:01:39.331 --> 00:01:40.933
and I knew
the William Shatner show

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:44.670
'cause they did the -- or the
older one, the original ser--

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.806
So, I was expecting it to be
that, but it wasn't that at all

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:47.874
so it scared
the [bleep] out of me.

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:48.975
-[ Chuckles ] Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.809
Now, I worry, 'cause,
like, you know,

00:01:50.809 --> 00:01:52.378
this film, you know, I --

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:53.780
My oldest is almost seven.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.981
And I feel like
I've done a bad job.

00:01:55.981 --> 00:01:57.917
We haven't shown them
enough movies yet,

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.319
and so they scare very easily

00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:01.787
'cause I don't think they
understand the stakes.

00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:03.689
We were showing them
the first "Paddington,"

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:06.692
and there's a moment
where Paddington --

00:02:06.692 --> 00:02:08.761
I hope I'm not ruining it.
-No, I -- No.

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:09.996
[ Laughter ]

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:11.530
-There's a scene where
Paddington has --

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.032
they, like, kick him
out of the house,

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:15.734
and my oldest
was, like, overcome with grief.

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:16.936
And I realized, "Oh, we haven't

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:18.370
taught them that,
like, Paddington's" --

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.239
I was like, "Paddington's
going to be fine."

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:23.543
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.
-So, is this -- I mean,

00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:26.512
is this a film -- have you seen
kids see this new film yet?

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:27.913
-No. No.

00:02:27.913 --> 00:02:30.916
I invited kids from my building
to the premiere part --

00:02:30.916 --> 00:02:32.385
[ Audience awws ]

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:34.487
[ Laughter ]

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:36.456
-He charged them for tickets.

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:37.990
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.925
-Then I harassed them
the whole time.

00:02:39.925 --> 00:02:41.060
They didn't like it.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.995
And they had a great time.
-That's great.

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:46.132
-My dad actually recorded --
he showed --

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:47.467
this is a non-sequitur,

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.502
but he showed me "Old Yeller"
when I was young

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:51.304
and Old Yeller gets shot.
-Yeah.

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.306
-But they get another dog.
-Yeah.

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:57.977
-He would record a tape on the
VHS, put the tab over the thing,

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.545
give it to friends of ours,

00:02:59.545 --> 00:03:02.215
but cut the movie...
-That's nice.

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:04.250
-...when he shoots the dog
and then give it to people.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.352
So you never saw --
-I remember being very upset

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.454
the first time
I saw "Old Yeller"

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:10.356
and taking it out on my parents
for not...

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.659
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-And I remember --

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:15.661
You know, my dad was making a
point, but you know the story is

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:18.698
about that, like, one, you can
replace a dog and you move on.

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:19.832
-Right, right.
-And I was like,

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:22.235
"Yeah, but they didn't
have to shoot it."

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:23.636
-Right, right, right.
-There's another version

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.638
where the dog just gets old.
-Right, right, right. Yeah.

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:27.406
They didn't have to go so hard
with the rabies, right?

00:03:27.406 --> 00:03:30.542
Doesn't it get rabies?
-They went hard in rabies, yeah.

00:03:30.542 --> 00:03:32.912
[ Laughter ]
-It was a trope at the time.

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:35.281
-Yeah, rabies was --
Remember simpler times?

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:36.482
When you were a kid,

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:37.583
you were just worried about,
like, rabies.

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:38.951
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:41.754
Actually, in this movie
we have a quicksand sequence,

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:44.090
which I remember --
which maybe I shouldn't say.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.560
But when I was a kid,
there was quicksand everywhere.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.096
Quicksand was very popular.
-Yeah, no. I think you're right.

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:51.831
-So much to the point
I was worried

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:53.833
that I had to worry
about quicksand when I...

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:56.035
-And I'm guessing
you didn't grow up in a place

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:57.035
with a lot of quicksand.

00:03:57.035 --> 00:03:58.304
-No, no, no. Indiana.

00:03:58.304 --> 00:03:59.838
-Yeah.
-No quicksand.

00:03:59.838 --> 00:04:03.042
-I think they're at almost zero
deaths a year from quicksand.

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.777
-[ Laughs ] Yeah, yeah.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:08.013
-Was that -- When you shoot
a quicksand scene, is it --

00:04:08.013 --> 00:04:09.749
I mean, obviously it's not
practical quicksand,

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:11.884
but I'm assuming
you can't CGI quicksand.

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:13.152
You've got to be in something.

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:15.221
-Yeah, they put, like,
a bunch of cork particles.

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:16.455
That's what they fill --
-Oh, interesting.

00:04:16.455 --> 00:04:18.424
-But it's very nice.
It's, like, very heated.

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:19.625
-Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:20.759
So, you're saying this might be,

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:22.695
10 years from now,
a spa treatment?

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:23.996
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:27.700
Yeah, it's the quicksand
close-to-death spa treatment.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.804
-Yeah, I did a quicksand rinse,
and I look and feel great.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:32.838
-Yeah.

00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:34.574
My hair is different.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:37.042
-You know, "Jurassic Park,"

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:38.177
was that a movie you saw
when you were...?

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:39.311
-Oh, yeah.
"Jurassic Park" was a big one.

00:04:39.311 --> 00:04:40.513
-That's fun.
And I think, like,

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:42.114
dinosaurs in general,
because it's like --

00:04:42.114 --> 00:04:43.315
You mentioned you watched
"Star Trek"

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:45.785
before you went to see
"Star Trek," the TV show.

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:48.387
I think the nice thing for kids
is, like, they all --

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:50.756
like, you don't have to
introduce dinosaurs to them.

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:52.124
Like, they just know dinosaurs.

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:53.792
It's so funny how much my kids
will just come home

00:04:53.792 --> 00:04:55.261
and tell me about
a new dinosaur,

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:57.063
and I remember the same thing,
and I'm like, "When does...?"

00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:58.965
Like, at some point, just other
kids on the playground

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:01.066
are like, "Hey."
-Right. Right.

00:05:01.066 --> 00:05:03.569
-"I wanna fill -- There's
a lot of different dinosaurs."

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.304
-That actually was going on
during the --

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:08.774
I got this during the first --
the first part of the pandemic

00:05:08.774 --> 00:05:10.910
when everyone was isolated.
-Yeah.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.413
-And so I was with my family
all the time,

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:16.716
and my son was just telling me
everything about every dinosaur.

00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:18.517
I knew the basics -- T. rex

00:05:18.517 --> 00:05:20.052
and everything that was
in "Jurassic Park."

00:05:20.052 --> 00:05:22.187
But apart from that --
So, when I got this...

00:05:22.187 --> 00:05:26.159
And obviously the themes are
obvious in relationship to COVID

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:28.661
that everyone's kind of facing
this thing that they don't know.

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:31.597
It's this invisible kind of
thing, but it was also big.

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:34.500
It was like dinosaurs and laser
guns, and he's into dinosaurs.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.402
I'm like, "I'm in." You know?
-Yeah.

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:38.571
-And then we went,
and he kind of --

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:39.872
he kind of knew everything.

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.541
The guy was,
like, introducing, like,

00:05:41.541 --> 00:05:43.276
"Oh, this is going to be --
might be a little scary.

00:05:43.276 --> 00:05:44.644
Like, don't be scared."
He's like, "No."

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:46.679
He's like, "Gallimimus. Paleo--"

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:49.248
He was -- You know,
"An eoraptor."

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:52.051
He was naming everything that --
He was really into it.

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:53.820
But he doesn't want to see the
movie 'cause he's going to be --

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.187
it's too scary.
-Really?

00:05:55.187 --> 00:05:57.690
-So, I basically made this thing
for him to watch

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:58.924
that he has no interest
in watching.

00:05:58.924 --> 00:06:00.259
-Are you --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.562
Is any part of you worried
that the "I'm too scared"

00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:04.964
is just a cover for the fact

00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:06.999
that he's not a huge fan
of yours as an actor?

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:08.634
-Yeah. Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:12.271
I tell him, like, "Oh, we're
gonna do movies sometimes."

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:13.906
Like, "Oh, that's cool.
I hate movies."

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:15.874
[ Laughter ]
He hates movies.

00:06:15.874 --> 00:06:18.110
All of his friends watch --
-So, is it just --

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.878
Like, does he just get anxiety
being in a big room?

00:06:19.878 --> 00:06:21.547
-He hates them.

00:06:21.547 --> 00:06:24.016
We showed -- I finally
showed him "Mary Poppins"

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:26.685
as the first movie, and
he's like, "Eh, it was okay."

00:06:26.685 --> 00:06:28.821
We're like, "Do you want to
watch anything else?"

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:30.289
He's like,
"No, I'm not interested."

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:33.559
-We also showed our kids
"Mary Poppins" during --

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:35.861
Can I make...
-This is the original one.

00:06:35.861 --> 00:06:38.564
-Yeah. The original for me.
Can I make an unpopular opinion?

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:40.066
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Could have used a cut. Right?

00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:41.133
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:42.602
-"Mary Poppins"
is a little bit --

00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:44.570
There's stuff you don't remember
from "Mary Poppins."

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.972
-It's pretty wild,
"Mary Poppins."

00:06:46.972 --> 00:06:49.175
-It's pretty wild.
-The whole sequence when

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:51.677
the guy is laughing and he goes
up to the top of the thing.

00:06:51.677 --> 00:06:54.247
-Yeah. It's terrifying for kids.
-Yeah, yeah. Or adults.

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:57.250
-It's very anti-laughter.
-Yeah, yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:58.517
-Alright.
I've got a ton more questions.

00:06:58.517 --> 00:07:00.520
We'll be right back
with more from Adam Driver.

